# CONTROL for do-block-on-inner-call.ex. The same meaning, spelled the way the
# language desugars it, and outliner gets it right.
#
# `defp f(x) do x end` IS `defp(f(x), do: x)` - elixir's do-block is sugar for
# a `do:` keyword argument on the outer call. This file writes the desugared
# form, and it contains the same inner call `f(x)` in the same position:
#
#   call [0, 17)
#     target: identifier  "defp"
#     arguments [4, 17)
#       call [5, 9)        <- the inner call, present here too
#       keywords [11, 16)  <- what the do-block means
#
# So the inner call is not what confuses the press. What confuses it is the
# token `do`: with a comma and a keyword there is nothing to shift into `f(x)`,
# the outer argument list simply continues, and both parsers agree.
#
# Read this beside the guilty witness and the pair says exactly where the
# defect lives - not in the construct, not in the nesting, but in one shift
# decision on one terminal.
roots 1
mends 0
holds keywords
lacks do_block
spans arguments 4 17
spans call 5 9
